<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luigi Palma di Cesnola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1832–1904</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luigi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a> di Cesnola was an Italian-born soldier, diplomat and collector. After serving in the Union army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>, he became American consul in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, where from 1865 to 1876 he dug at many ancient sites and assembled a huge collection of Cypriot antiquities, including the so-called Curium treasure, whose findspots have long been doubted. The Metropolitan Museum of Art bought the collection, and he became its first director in 1879.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luigi-palma-di-cesnola.jpg" alt="Luigi Palma di Cesnola" width="260" height="338" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luigi_P._di_Cesnola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
